city council Blas Fernandez Cheyenne Wyoming well I don't know where to start I'm hearing a lot of things out there and there's obviously some confusion so I respect what Jo has done and Jo is a great partner with the city but I do want to clarify some things in common said he said this is let's start with the residential I'm sorry the mechanical commissioning deals with energy efficiency has absolutely nothing to do with public safety if we through the energy code away the buildings would be safe they may not be as efficient when it comes to energy consumption but they will be safe has nothing to do with carbon monoxide the reason I included this amendment excuse me was because I've had mechanical contractors come to me and

say this is an unneeded cost for smaller commercial buildings that just used used rooftop units you can easily build a small building under 15,000 or close to 15,000 but then you're adding a lot of cost just for the testing commissioning means testing you test the equipment to make sure it's working efficiently that that comes into play with larger buildings where you have complex systems and all I can tell you is mechanical systems can be very complex so this or this code requirement typically requires an engineer to come in and test the equipment to make sure it's working fine if you guys believe that it's better to test everything that will drive up the cost because some of these buildings will require additional engineering services to test the equipment and

it's a fairly straightforward process to just mount rooftop units on like a big-box store like a office depot something like that okay so now I want to get to the main issue and there seems to be a lot of confusion between what is a home and what is a multi-family dwelling there are two building codes in the United States there's the IRC which is a one in two family dwelling code pretty straightforward okay that would be the Home Builders Association anybody who builds homes when you say the word home to me that means either a one family home or a twin home like a duplex that's under the IRC the other code is the code for anything larger than that which would be a three family dwelling or larger that that is a multi-family home that is a commercial building under the IBC two different animals two different buildings two different standards they don't really talk to each other that much but I just wanted to clear that up because I'm hearing homes my intent is not to put fire sprinkler in homes I believe when that

came into be when the 2009 IRC was created they the fire officials got together and they voted to put that into the code however what happened after that was most of the communities and including states decided that they were not going to enforce that so at this point in time I don't I am NOT seeking residential sprinklers I mean that's that's I don't see that in the near future okay so where does that leave us with the four plexes and the other multi-family dwellings multifamily dwellings three or more under the commercial IBC okay so this community I'll tell you right up front is the only community that I know that does not require fire sprinklers in multi-family homes okay

and I've worked in many different communities for different states I was surprised to see it I don't know how that happened well I know is that when I talk to the fire officials here they were against it and they asked me if I would join them and trying to change that I said I would be happy to I think I think it makes sense now there's two things going on here I'm hearing cost cost is an important thing okay when you when you want to build affordable apartments that's that that can be an important important thing but to say well you know I think we can bypass the sprinklers and save a few dollars but in the end where does that leave us and

this is my true concern okay I I'm not so worried about the cost I'm not so worried about how these buildings are going to be built I'm worried about the liability because as a building official I have been involved in lawsuits and it always boils down to did you or did you not follow the code well when you make an amendment to the code and you change it my concern is what what the madam attorney said are we really following the intent of the code is this what the state statute reads I've had discussions with the state at AGG and the State Fire Marshall unfortunately they will not give me anything in writing and they refer me back to the city attorney so

somehow some some way I think we can come to some kind of a definition of what the state statute reads unfortunately I am not in a position to interpret a statute all I know is that I believe to keep the consumer the homeowner the renter saved from a fire a fire sprinkler goes a long way now you can use the argument that we can bypass the sprinklers and nothing is going to happen well nothing may happen do things happen in schools do things happen in hospitals nursing homes I mean you can use that argument for any type of building that typically requires a fire sprinkler but the bottom line is the the legal process will take over eventually if somebody gets hurt injured or god forbid if somebody dies there's going to be a lawsuit and

I've seen how attorneys work and if you misinterpret of law or you or you do it wrong you can be held liable and I just want to be here for the record of saying that I support sprinklers and I respectfully leave these decisions to the council I know that's your job I don't pretend to tell you guys how to do your job all I can tell you is what I know and what I see what the code means the IRC is not the IBC single family homes which include one family and to family dwellings is not what we're talking about here we're talking about multi-family homes these are apartment buildings these belong in the commercial arena and

these are the IBC and the way I see it at the state statute requires sprinklers I don't know how else to put it I hope I try to clarify some of these issues city of July requires sprinklers for four plexes city of Laramie requires sprinklers in basements in homes they're coming I talked to the building official in Sheridan Kevin bear mr. kevin bear they're trying to get sprinklers in single-family homes I told them good luck with that but the movement is is coming sooner or later it will be here it'll probably happen when I retire but there is a movement to put sprinklers and why because when you have a fire let me give you a real simple example my my mom is is getting older and

she still lives at home and she can't move very fast if you if she had a fire she could possibly be trapped in a building and if and if there were sprinkler the sprinkler is designed to put out the fire allow time for people to react to it and then you know get her out firewall is not going to do that a firewall is designed to just prevent the fire from spreading to other parts of the building so so fire sprinklers in the most simplest terms how preserved life and give people a chance to get out of a building and that's what I'm thinking about when I think of the IBC and what the code requires and

